    "media:imagetext": "October 15, 1937\nDM 27/\nPERSONAL AND CONFIDENTIAL\nMr. Harold M. Bixby\nVice President for China\nPan American Airways Co.\nManila, Philippine Isis.\nDear Mr. Bixby:\nIour personal and confidential letter of October 7th\narrived on the same day with your cable #148 outlining your suggestions\nfor a resumption of cooperation with CNAC. As you will have observed\nand so it is hardly calculated to be helpful but, as nothing is pre-\nFrankly I think you described\nyour progran perfectly\nwhen you called it a forlorn hope and referred to it as visionary. I\ncan hardly believe for a moment that it will work out in actual prec-\ntice, for it seems to me that the seme reasons will militate ageinst\nit that forced us to drop out of the picture two months ago. However,\nyou must have some good reason for feeling that the situation is more\nhopeful on. Of course,\nif we can hold our position by some tenuous\ntie-up with our Chinese\nfriends it may be helpful when the day of set-\ntlement arrives, but I recall your saying sometime ago that any effort\nto cooperate or participate in the operations of CNAC under present\nconditions night simply result in our being declared \"in\" when the\nfinal probable debacle comes, naking it even more difficult for us to\ntry to revert to a settlement on the basis of the status quo of August\nthirteenth. It seems to me that in the end our settlement is very\nlikely to be arranged through some form of diplomatic claim, and I\ndoubt whether our chances there would be notably improved by our re-\nentry into the picture at this time. However, the President feels that\nfrom the lashington angle it would be a very good thing if we could con-\ntinue to hold some sort of à position in China.\nHe also had in mind the possibility that we might work\nout some further financing from the Iport and Export Bank. 1h18 was\na new angle to me and apparently his thoughts in this line are based\non some very informal conversations he had in fashington. Nevertheless",
